NAME
    sh - execute bounded command scripts, logical lists, and pipelines

SYNOPSIS
    sh [FILE | -]
    COMMAND [ARG...] [< FILE] [> FILE | >> FILE]
    COMMAND... | COMMAND...
    PIPELINE [&& PIPELINE | || PIPELINE]...

DESCRIPTION
    With FILE, sh reads one UTF-8 script from the read-only filesystem service.
    With - or no operand, it reads standard input. Blank lines and lines whose
    first non-whitespace byte is # are ignored. Every other physical line uses
    the normal TROE command grammar and is executed sequentially in the owning
    shell session after the complete script has been validated.

    Script staging is transactional: malformed, excessive, failed, or faulted
    sh.kex execution runs none of the submitted commands. Runtime command
    failures do not stop later lines, and the script returns the last executed
    command's status. A cd line changes the owning shell's logical directory.

    Source is limited to 64 KiB, one physical command line to 512 UTF-8 bytes,
    and one batch to 1,024 submitted lines. Nesting is limited to four scripts
    within one shared 1,024-pipeline execution budget; every logical-list entry
    consumes that budget even when short-circuited. Text scripts are not
    directly executable and have no shebang handling; use sh FILE.

    A bare ordinary command resolves only /bin/COMMAND.kex. A command token
    containing / instead names an exact KEX package path relative to the
    current directory or absolute from /. No suffix is inferred and neither
    PATH nor the current directory is searched implicitly. The target must be a
    regular file containing a valid KEX package. Package capabilities are still
    limited by the launcher; copying a package does not add authority.
    Direct interactive execution outside /bin asks for confirmation and treats
    Enter or any answer other than y as cancellation. The prompt is an advisory
    provenance warning, not a filesystem permission or package-trust decision.

    Single and double quotes group literal text; neither performs expansion.
    Unquoted && and || have equal precedence, associate left to right, and run
    the next pipeline after success or non-success respectively. An unquoted |
    connects bounded byte streams. Unquoted < supplies the first stage from
    FILE. Unquoted > truncates or creates FILE before execution and streams
    final stdout, while >> creates or streams at the existing end.

    File streams use a 16 KiB default working buffer and are not capped by the
    shell. A failing command may leave an empty file or written prefix. The
    1 MiB limit applies only between sequential pipeline stages. Redirection
    paths do not enter command argv. Variables, expansion, command substitution,
    control-flow blocks, stderr redirection, heredocs, jobs, descriptor numbers,
    and multiline grammar are not supported by the current script grammar.

EXAMPLES
    sh /share/sh/bench.sh
    printf 'echo from-stdin\n' | sh -
    cp /bin/echo.kex /vol/shared/echo-copy
    cd /vol/shared
    ./echo-copy from-shared
    Run untrusted application './echo-copy' outside /bin? [y/N] y
